Chapter: RocAliasOriginal
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 Ported from roc-lang/roc, src/eval/test/eval_closure_recursion_tests.zig,
 "inspect: list alias return original after aliasing", case 10 in file order:

     { x = [1, 2, 3] _y = x match x { [a, b, c] => a + b + c, _ => 0 } }

 and Roc expects 6.

 The CONTROL for `roc-alias-list`, and Roc keeps both. Same two bindings, same
 list, same answer; the one respect that differs is WHICH NAME is read. If the
 alias is a copy rather than a share, one of the pair still passes and the
 other does not, which is why the pair is worth more than either half.

 Roc marks the unread binding `_y`; Codex has no discard sigil for a `let`, so
 the binding is named plainly and simply not read. That is the one adaptation
 with any content in it -- an unused `let` is a thing a backend may drop
 entirely, and dropping it is exactly what this case is checking does not
 change the answer.

 Adaptations otherwise: list patterns become `list-at`, and `6.0` is `6`.

Section: Functions

  alias-original : Integer -> Integer
  alias-original (n) =
   let x = [1, 2, 3]
   in let y = x
   in list-at x 0 + list-at x 1 + list-at x 2

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing
  opening = act
   print-line-uni (show (alias-original 0))
  end
